{"title":"PackRight","sub":"Should a packaging maker buy a plastics recycler?","type":"M&A","industry":"Industrials / packaging","geo":"Europe","diff":3,"fmt":"Interviewer-led","time":"35-40 min","skills":"Valuation with multiples · synergies · avoiding double counting","prompt":"PackRight makes plastic packaging for food and drink brands across Europe: revenue $800M, EBITDA $120M. New rules will require packaging to contain at least 30% recycled plastic by 2030. PackRight is considering buying RePlast, a recycler with revenue of $120M and EBITDA of $12M. The owners are asking $180M. Should PackRight buy?","clarify":[{"q":"Why buy, not buy on the market?","a":"PackRight worries it won't be able to buy enough recycled plastic at a reasonable price."},{"q":"PackRight's needs?","a":"Uses 400,000 tonnes of plastic a year → needs 120,000 tonnes recycled by 2030."},{"q":"RePlast output?","a":"80,000 tonnes a year, sold at market price (~$1,500/tonne) to many buyers."},{"q":"Typical valuation?","a":"Recyclers usually sell for 9-11× EBITDA. Use 10×."}],"structure":{"root":"Is RePlast worth more than $180M to PackRight?","branches":[{"head":"RePlast on its own","items":["Market & growth","Profitability & quality","Value at 10× EBITDA"]},{"head":"Synergies","items":["Cost: logistics, overheads","Revenue: 'green' contracts","Strategic: guaranteed supply"]},{"head":"Risks","items":["Integration & culture","Regulation changes","Technology / quality of recycled plastic"]},{"head":"Deal","items":["Price vs value","Alternatives: long-term contracts, build our own plant"]}]},"hypothesis":"At 15× EBITDA the asking price is high for a recycler. I expect the deal to make sense only if the supply-security benefit is real and large.","questions":[{"q":"Is there really a supply problem? Look at this market outlook.","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"373.551719pt\" height=\"196.548pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 373.551719 196.548\" xmlns=\"http: