515 L 1030 96 Q 1030 0 934 0 L 582 0 L 582 0 z\n\" transform=\"scale(0.015625)\"/><path id=\"LibreFranklin-Regular-46\" d=\"M 2906 0 Q 2861 0 2838 22 Q 2816 45 2816 90 L 2816 358 Q 2816 410 2793 416 Q 2771 422 2733 384 Q 2662 320 2531 211 Q 2400 102 2201 19 Q 2003 -64 1728 -64 Q 1325 -64 1005 128 Q 685 320 496 701 Q 307 1082 307 1645 Q 307 2189 496 2595 Q 685 3002 1027 3229 Q 1370 3456 1805 3456 Q 2118 3456 2329 3353 Q 2541 3251 2701 3117 Q 2758 3072 2780 3081 Q 2803 3091 2803 3162 L 2803 4666 Q 2803 4749 2874 4749 L 3264 4749 Q 3334 4749 3334 4666 L 3334 102 Q 3334 45 3315 22 Q 3296 0 3232 0 L 2906 0 L 2906 0 z\nM 2797 2643 Q 2598 2842 2393 2950 Q 2189 3059 1933 3059 Q 1626 3059 1379 2902 Q 1133 2746 995 2432 Q 858 2118 858 1664 Q 858 1197 998 899 Q 1139 602 1376 464 Q 1613 326 1894 326 Q 2157 326 2358 448 Q 2560 570 2678 749 Q 2797 928 2797 1101 L 2797 2643 L 2797 2643 z\n\" transform=\"scale(0.015625)\"/></defs><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-d\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-48\" transform=\"translate(72.203125 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-5b\" transform=\"translate(130.296875 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-3b\" transform=\"translate(220.890625 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-5c\" transform=\"translate(275.6875 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-46\" transform=\"translate(334.09375 0)\"/></g></g></g></g></g><defs><clipPath id=\"p0949c2da26\"><rect x=\"20.391719\" y=\"27.296\" width=\"345.96\" height=\"144.144\"/></clipPath></defs></svg>\n","title":"Exhibit 1 · European recycled plastic, million tonnes (case data)","note":null},"answer":"<p>Supply exceeds demand until 2027. From <b>2028 demand overtakes supply</b>, and by 2030 the gap is 1.9Mt (−20%). <b>So-what:</b> recycled plastic will be scarce just as PackRight legally needs it. Expect prices to rise, and some buyers to get no supply at all. Securing supply is valuable.</p>"},{"q":"A colleague says: 'RePlast's plastic costs $1,200/tonne to make and sells for $1,500. If we own it, we save $300 × 80,000 = $24M a year. That's a huge synergy.' Do you agree?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<p><b>No, that's double counting.</b> RePlast already earns that $300/tonne margin. It's part of the $12M EBITDA we're paying for. Moving the profit from RePlast to PackRight doesn't create new profit for the group.</p> <p>The real value is <b>access</b>: in a shortage, owning RePlast guarantees 80k of the 120k tonnes needed, and protects PackRight against price spikes and lost contracts.</p>","note":"This is the key test of the case. Don't hint unless the candidate is completely stuck."},{"q":"Now value the deal. Real synergies: $5M a year of cost savings (logistics, overheads), and brands will pay a 3% premium for guaranteed-recycled packaging on $200M of PackRight sales, about $6M of extra EBITDA. Integration costs $15M one-off.","exhibit":null,"answer":"<table class=\"data mini\"><tr><td>RePlast standalone: 12 × 10</td><td class=\"r\">$120M</td></tr> <tr><td>Cost synergies: 5 × 10</td><td class=\"r\">$50M</td></tr> <tr><td>Revenue synergies: 6 × 10</td><td class=\"r\">$60M</td></tr> <tr><td>Integration cost</td><td class=\"r\">−$15M</td></tr> <tr class=\"total\"><td>Maximum value to PackRight</td><td class=\"r\">$215M</td></tr></table> <p>$215M > $180M asking → <b>$35M of value created</b>. But <b>without</b> the revenue synergy: 120 + 50 − 15 = <b>$155M &lt; $180M</b>.</p> <p><b>So-what:</b> the whole decision depends on whether customers really pay the green premium, which is the least certain synergy.</p>"},{"q":"How would you protect PackRight in the deal?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<ul><li>Negotiate the upfront price towards ~$155-165M (what's justified without the revenue synergy)</li> <li>Pay an <b>earn-out</b> (extra payment later) only if green-premium contracts are signed</li> <li>Before signing, get letters of intent from 2-3 major brands for the premium packaging</li> <li>Check RePlast's technology can produce food-grade recycled plastic</li></ul>"}],"reco":"PackRight should buy RePlast, but not at a flat $180M. Recycled plastic will be scarce from 2028, and RePlast covers two-thirds of what PackRight legally needs by 2030. With all synergies, RePlast is worth about $215M to PackRight. Without the uncertain green premium, it's worth only $155M. So I'd offer around $160M upfront plus an earn-out of up to $20M tied to signed premium contracts. Key risk: integration and food-grade quality. Next steps: technical due diligence and talks with three anchor customers.","great":["Catches the $24M double count","Separates certain synergies from uncertain ones","Structures the price (earn-out) instead of giving a plain yes or no","Connects the regulation deadline to the shortage"],"id":4,"slug":"packright"}